WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.920 --> 00:00:08.364
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:09.951 --> 00:00:12.200
DAVE WILLIAMS: Frequently,
when you look at a problem,

00:00:12.200 --> 00:00:16.000
you may have questions about
various groupings within your data.

00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:18.430
For example, is there a
difference between new staff

00:00:18.430 --> 00:00:21.240
members vs. experienced staff?

00:00:21.240 --> 00:00:24.480
Do we see a difference between
shifts or by clinical training?

00:00:24.480 --> 00:00:27.040
Nurse vs. physician vs. paramedic?

00:00:27.040 --> 00:00:31.150
This desire to look at the
population as various subgroups is

00:00:31.150 --> 00:00:32.680
called stratification.

00:00:32.680 --> 00:00:36.820
By stratifying your data,
you may be able to identify

00:00:36.820 --> 00:00:40.090
opportunities for improvement
that aren't obvious from the data

00:00:40.090 --> 00:00:41.190
as a whole.

00:00:41.190 --> 00:00:42.960
I worked with a group
of pharmacists that

00:00:42.960 --> 00:00:45.610
were trying to improve the
process known as medication

00:00:45.610 --> 00:00:48.150
reconciliation in hospital.

00:00:48.150 --> 00:00:52.470
Medication reconciliation is the process
of creating the most accurate list

00:00:52.470 --> 00:00:55.950
possible of all medications a
patient is taking and comparing

00:00:55.950 --> 00:00:57.960
that list against the
physician's orders.

00:00:57.960 --> 00:01:00.970
The goal is to provide
correct medications

00:01:00.970 --> 00:01:04.090
to the patient at all transition
points within the hospital.

00:01:04.090 --> 00:01:06.500
As the pharmacists were
discussing the process,

00:01:06.500 --> 00:01:09.030
they quickly identified
different scenarios

00:01:09.030 --> 00:01:11.640
within the process
worthy of stratifying,

00:01:11.640 --> 00:01:15.470
including day versus night
and weekday versus weekend.

00:01:15.470 --> 00:01:19.630
It was predicted there were variations
in the process in these areas

00:01:19.630 --> 00:01:21.990
that would be worth understanding.

00:01:21.990 --> 00:01:25.690
So let's take a look at
an example of that data.

00:01:25.690 --> 00:01:27.910
Let's imagine that their
prediction came true.

00:01:27.910 --> 00:01:30.790
So if we were going to draw
ourselves a little bit of a run

00:01:30.790 --> 00:01:35.830
chart, and we come down here.

00:01:35.830 --> 00:01:38.500
And imagine we had our
days of the week, right.

00:01:38.500 --> 00:01:47.670
So we have Monday, Tuesday, Wednesday,
Thursday, Friday, Saturday, Sunday,

00:01:47.670 --> 00:01:54.470
Monday, Tuesday, Wednesday,
Thursday, Friday, Saturday, Sunday.

00:01:54.470 --> 00:01:55.630
So we got two weeks here.

00:01:55.630 --> 00:01:58.390
And imagine we're coming along
and we're plotting our data

00:01:58.390 --> 00:02:04.740
and on Monday and Tuesday the
data is kind of right here.

00:02:04.740 --> 00:02:05.580
Natural variation.

00:02:05.580 --> 00:02:08.300
But then all of a sudden we
look up and on the weekend

00:02:08.300 --> 00:02:12.980
we have a change in our data
points that seem really different.

00:02:12.980 --> 00:02:15.550
It's almost like there's
a different system that's

00:02:15.550 --> 00:02:17.780
occurring on the weekends.

00:02:17.780 --> 00:02:25.410
These examples here are a different
system than the ones down here below.

00:02:25.410 --> 00:02:30.070
And so this is a very common example
of where you might look at a place

00:02:30.070 --> 00:02:36.380
where it makes sense to stratify out
the two different parts of the week

00:02:36.380 --> 00:02:39.460
because you're seeing that there
are different systems that exist.

00:02:39.460 --> 00:02:44.640
Another common example is to create a
small multiples display of measures.

00:02:44.640 --> 00:02:48.010
For example, imagine we're
thinking about hand-washing.

00:02:48.010 --> 00:02:53.240
So we may want to look at our data
overall and have a run chart that

00:02:53.240 --> 00:02:55.170
tells us about our overall data.

00:02:58.260 --> 00:02:59.760
So overall.

00:02:59.760 --> 00:03:02.560
But we may have a
prediction that there is

00:03:02.560 --> 00:03:05.800
some variation across
our different sites,

00:03:05.800 --> 00:03:10.305
and so we might have several different
places that we're looking at.

00:03:13.060 --> 00:03:19.370
And we'll just call 'em
like A, B, C, D, and E.

00:03:19.370 --> 00:03:22.590
And we'll stratify those
individual units out--

00:03:22.590 --> 00:03:25.300
or places-- so that we
can look at their data

00:03:25.300 --> 00:03:31.300
and see is there variation
that exists across sites?

00:03:31.300 --> 00:03:33.350
And the stratification
helps us potentially

00:03:33.350 --> 00:03:36.330
see where there's opportunity
for improvement that

00:03:36.330 --> 00:03:38.200
will help change our outcome.

00:03:38.200 --> 00:03:41.660
So by doing this, it
enables us to be able to see

00:03:41.660 --> 00:03:43.570
where there's variation in the system.

00:03:43.570 --> 00:03:47.410
When you think about your work, what
are some of the obvious stratification

00:03:47.410 --> 00:03:52.450
opportunities you see that might help
you learn more about your process?

